 1 big thing: Meet the flying medic 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Photo: Pivotal A paramedic responding to a recent 911 call in coastal North Carolina piloted a "Jetsons"-like aircraft to the scene, Axios' Joann Muller reports. 

 It landed right in the patient's yard, about 20 minutes before ground ambulances arrived. That fast airborne response offers a glimpse of the future of emergency medical care in rural America, where hospitals are far away and time is critical. 🚁 Hyde County officials say they're the first in the U.S. to use an eVTOL (electric vertical takeoff and landing aircraft) for real 911 calls.

 Flight-trained paramedic Quinn Reece is flying a BlackFly ultralight plane, carrying first-response gear to help stabilize patients before ground EMTs can get to the scene. The Pivotal BlackFly in flight. Photo: Pivotal The single-passenger BlackFly is a simple, lightweight design.

 It can take off and land vertically on pavement, dirt or grass. If something goes wrong, a parachute can carry the 254-pound aircraft safely to the ground. 🚑 Reece said her biggest takeaway from the project is "seeing how these new aircraft can change the way we think about emergency response, especially in rural communities."

